+\begin{lemma}[Bounded perfect fractional matchings]
+\label{lem:selection:bounded-fractional-matching}
+Let $0<\beta<1/2$ and let $m\ge4/\beta$ be an integer.
+Every $m$-vertex graph $Q$ with $\delta(Q)\ge(1/2+\beta)m$
+has a perfect fractional matching $\lambda$ satisfying
+\[
+ \frac{\beta}{5m}\le\lambda(e)\le\frac5{\beta m}
+ \qquad(e\in E(Q)).
+\]
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+Let $B$ be the bipartite graph with parts
+$V_L=\{v_L:v\in V(Q)\}$ and $V_R=\{v_R:v\in V(Q)\}$,
+where $u_Lv_R\in E(B)$ if and only if $uv\in E(Q)$.
+Both parts have size $m$, and $\delta(B)\ge(1/2+\beta)m$.
+Put $t=\lfloor\beta m/2\rfloor$, so that
+$\beta m/4\le t\le\beta m/2$.
+A greedy proper edge-colouring partitions $E(B)$ into at most
+$2m-1$ matchings. Partition each nonempty colour class into
+matchings of size at most $t$, obtaining an edge partition
+$M_1,\ldots,M_K$. Since $e(B)\le m^2$, we have
+\[
+ m/2\le\Delta(B)\le K
+ \le2m-1+e(B)/t\le5m/\beta.
+\]
+For each $i\in[K]$, put $a_i=|M_i|$ and $B_i=B-V(M_i)$.
+Both parts of $B_i$ have size $m-a_i$.
+We choose $t$ pairwise edge-disjoint perfect matchings
+$N_{i,1},\ldots,N_{i,t}$ in $B_i$.
+After choosing the first $j-1$ matchings, where $j\in[t]$,
+the remaining graph has minimum degree at least
+\[
+ (1/2+\beta)m-a_i-(j-1)\ge m/2\ge(m-a_i)/2.
+\]
+Hall's theorem therefore gives the next perfect matching.
+Thus $F_{i,j}=M_i\cup N_{i,j}$ is a perfect matching of $B$.
+For $e\in E(B)$, define
+\[
+ y(e)=\frac1{Kt}\sum_{i=1}^K\sum_{j=1}^t
+       \mathbf1_{\{e\in F_{i,j}\}}.
+\]
+Then $\sum_{e\in E_B(z)}y(e)=1$ for every $z\in V(B)$.
+Every edge $e$ belongs to exactly one $M_i$, and hence to all
+$t$ matchings $F_{i,1},\ldots,F_{i,t}$.
+For each other index $i'$, it belongs to at most one $N_{i',j}$.
+Consequently
+\[
+ \frac{\beta}{5m}\le\frac1K\le y(e)
+ \le\frac1K+\frac1t
+ \le\frac2m+\frac4{\beta m}\le\frac5{\beta m}.
+\]
+For $uv\in E(Q)$, put
+$\lambda(uv)=(y(u_Lv_R)+y(v_Lu_R))/2$.
+These weights satisfy the required bounds, and for every $v\in V(Q)$,
+\[
+ \sum_{u\in N_Q(v)}\lambda(uv)
+ =\frac12\left(\sum_{u\in N_Q(v)}y(v_Lu_R)
+              +\sum_{u\in N_Q(v)}y(u_Lv_R)\right)=1.
+\]
+\end{proof}
